    name: 'How to Use a Quitclaim Deed in a Divorce',
    description:
      "A quitclaim deed transfers one spouse's ownership interest in real property to the other during or after a divorce. The departing spouse signs the deed as grantor, the deed is notarized and recorded with the county, and the mortgage is handled separately via refinance.",
    totalTime: 'PT3H',
    tool: [
      { '@type': 'HowToTool', name: 'Quitclaim Deed Form' },
      { '@type': 'HowToTool', name: 'Notary Public' },
    ],
    estimatedCost: {
      '@type': 'MonetaryAmount',
      currency: 'USD',
      value: '14.99',
      description: 'Quitclaim deed form — plus county recording fees ($10–$30 typical)',
    },
    step: [
      {
        '@type': 'HowToStep',
        position: 1,
        name: 'Confirm the divorce decree specifies who gets the property',
        text: 'Review your divorce decree or marital settlement agreement to confirm which spouse retains the property. The deed cannot be prepared until ownership is established in writing by agreement or court order.',
      },
      {
        '@type': 'HowToStep',
        position: 2,
        name: 'The departing spouse signs the quitclaim deed as grantor',
        text: "The spouse giving up their ownership interest (grantor) signs the deed transferring their interest to the remaining spouse (grantee). The deed must include both parties' full legal names and addresses, plus the property's legal description from the existing deed.",
      },
